<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime">
  <!--
    Beginner-friendly English tooltips for the launcher UI.
    To translate later (Japanese, etc.), copy this file to Tooltips.ja.xaml, translate the string
    values (keep the x:Key names), and swap the merged dictionary in App.xaml.
  -->

  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.Profile">A cab profile is one game folder (one cabinet). You can keep a separate profile for your Test folder, your main cab, or P2–P4.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ProfileNew">Open the setup wizard to create a new cab profile. It walks through the game folder, card, unique serial, network, card server, and checks. A new profile gets a random unique serial automatically. If the folder already has its own unique serial, that serial is kept.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ProfileDelete">Remove this profile from the list. The game folder and its files are not deleted.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ProfileSave">Save all profiles to the JSON file next to this launcher. Card values are never stored there.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ProfileStore">Where your profiles are saved on this PC.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StartAll">Start this cab in the right order: patched card server first (and wait until it is listening), then the Live Monitor if ticked, then the game. It never starts the pack's stock Server.exe.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.Cancel">Stop a start that is still in progress. Programs that already started keep running; use a Stop button to close them.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.CheckPort">Show what is listening on the card server port (usually 28080). Useful to see whether the patched server, the stock server, or something else is using it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StopAll">Close the card server, Live Monitor, and game that belong to this profile. Other cab folders are left alone. Don't do this in the middle of a match on this card server.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StartCard">Start only the patched card server for this profile, and wait until its port is listening.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StopCard">Stop only this profile's card server. Tracked by the process this launcher started, or by the Server-Infinite.exe in this profile's card-server folder — never a server from another folder.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StartLm">Start only the Live Monitor (vsac29_Release_LM.exe) in this profile's game folder.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StopLm">Stop only the Live Monitor that belongs to this profile (this folder's vsac29_Release_LM.exe). Other cab LMs are left alone.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StartClient">Start only the game (vsac29_Release.exe) in this profile's game folder.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StopClient">Stop only the game that belongs to this profile (this folder's vsac29_Release.exe). Other cab folders are left alone.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ComponentStatus">Whether this part is running for the selected profile. "This folder" means a matching program was found in this profile's folder, even if this launcher didn't start it in this session.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.Name">A name for this cabinet, for example "Cab 1" or "P2". It is stored in the launcher profile, shown in the window title and the log, and is only for you. The game does not use it and still names its own window (often PCB_xx).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GameFolder">The cabinet folder that has vsac29_Release.exe, config.ini, lmnfig.ini, and card.ini. Pick the folder for this cab, not a different P2/P3/P4 or Test copy unless you mean to.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GameFolderBrowse">Browse to the game (cab) folder.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.CardServerPath">The patched card server to start: 3-Start-Server.bat or Server-Infinite.exe from a current exvs2-matchmaking package (v0.4.0 or later). Never pick the pack's stock server\Server.exe.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.CardServerBrowse">Browse to 3-Start-Server.bat or Server-Infinite.exe. The pack's Server.exe is refused.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ServerArgs">Extra arguments for the card server. Leave this empty unless a guide tells you otherwise.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StartCardCheck">When you click Start (all), also start the patched card server if its port is free. If the patched server is already running, it is reused.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StartLmCheck">When you click Start (all), also start the Live Monitor (vsac29_Release_LM.exe). Tick this if this cab should host the LM.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.StartClientCheck">When you click Start (all), also start the game client (vsac29_Release.exe).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.CardServerHost">Usually 127.0.0.1 — the game talks to the card server on this PC.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.CardServerPort">The card server port. Typical value: 28080. The launcher waits until this port is listening before it starts the LM or the game.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.PortTimeout">How many seconds to wait for the card server to open its port. Typical value: 90.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.LmDelay">How many seconds to wait after starting the LM before starting the game. Typical value: 3. Increase this if the game starts too soon and cannot find the LM.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.Serial">A unique 4-character cabinet ID written to config.ini (serial =). It must not match anyone else on the matchmaker, or you will be treated as the same cab. A new profile gets one automatically. "New serial" is the only way to replace a unique serial that is already in the folder.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.NewSerial">Make a new random 4-character serial and write it to config.ini (a backup is made first). Only use this when you really want to change this cab's identity.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.Card">The emulated Banapass card in card.ini: a 20-digit (or 20-hex) access code plus a 32-character uppercase hex chip ID. The values are never shown here. Do not share them. Each cab should have its own card.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.NewCard">Create a brand-new card for this cab. The card server will see a new player on the next insert. The old card.ini is backed up first so you can go back. Never share the new values.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.AccessPrefix">Optional digits placed at the start of a new access code, for example 99 for a second-player cab. Leave empty for a fully random code. This does not change an existing card until you click New card.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.SyncSnow">Also update SnowLauncher.ini when this launcher changes the serial, card, or IPs, so opening SnowLauncher later does not put the old values back. Close SnowLauncher before writing. Sync never writes DisableSocketHook=True (SnowLauncher does that for the LM, which makes it grab every address).</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.AutoDetectIp">On every Start (all), fill the PCB (client) IP with this PC's LAN address. This never changes the LM IP — lmnfig.ini is the authority for the Live Monitor.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.DetectNow">Fill the PCB (client) IP with this PC's current LAN address now. The LM IP is left as it is.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.PcbIp">The address the game (the cab / PCB) uses, written to config.ini IpAddress. This is usually your PC's main LAN IP, for example 192.168.1.69. The game binds its ports on this address.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.LmIp">The address the Live Monitor uses, written to lmnfig.ini IpAddress. For one cab this can be the same as the PCB IP. If you run more than one cab or LM on this PC, pick a separate address on the same adapter (a secondary IPv4, for example 192.168.1.245). If the LM is pinned to the same specific address the game uses, you can get error 21-01 (live monitor disconnect). Never use 127.0.0.1 here — the game cannot reach an LM bound to loopback from a LAN address, which causes error 21-01. The list includes every local IPv4 address, including secondary ones. Auto-detect will not overwrite this.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WriteIps">Write the PCB IP to config.ini and the LM IP to lmnfig.ini (and to SnowLauncher.ini if that option is on). If Server= in those files is missing a port or does not match the card server address, you will be offered a fix — a wrong Server= causes LM error 21-32. The log shows a before-and-after diff. A backup is made if a file changes. Close SnowLauncher and the game first.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.CurrentIps">The addresses currently stored in the game files. config.ini is the PCB (client). lmnfig.ini is the LM and is authoritative — auto-detect will not change it. Server= in both files must be the card server host:port (usually 127.0.0.1:28080). A value with no port, or the LM's own IP, causes error 21-32. lmnfig.ini DisableSocketHook must stay False when more than one cab or LM runs on this PC — True makes the LM grab every address and other LMs fail with 21-32.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.Troubleshooting">Plain-English notes for common errors: 21-01 (LM disconnect), 21-32 (card server address or DisableSocketHook), card in use / このカードは使用中です, a missing card server, and firewall or TinyWall blocks.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardBack">Go back to the previous setup step. Nothing is written until you click Save.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardNext">Continue to the next setup step. Nothing is written until you click Save on the last page.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardCancel">Close the wizard without writing game files or saving a new profile.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardSave">Write the planned file changes (with backups) and save this profile. The game is not started.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardSaveStart">Write the planned file changes (with backups), save this profile, and start the card server, LM, and game in order.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardKeepCard">Leave the existing valid card.ini alone. The access code and chip ID stay hidden and are never logged.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardOnlyCab">This PC will run only one cab and one LM. They may share one IPv4 address. That is fine for a single-cab setup.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardMultiCab">This PC will run more than one cab or LM. Give the LM its own IPv4 on the same adapter (a secondary address). If none exists, the wizard shows a one-line elevated PowerShell command to add one. It does not run that command for you.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardAddIpCommand">Elevated PowerShell that adds a secondary IPv4 with -SkipAsSource, so Windows does not use the LM address as the default outbound IP. Copy and run it in an administrator PowerShell window; the launcher never runs it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardMatchUrl">The public address of the matchmaking server, for example 203.0.113.10:28090 or http://host:28090/match. The matchmaker is on 28090; your own card server (and its web page) is on 28080. This is written to the card server's Configurations/server.json as MatchUrl (and StunHost). Do not use 127.0.0.1. If you enter a VPS address with port 28080, the launcher warns you.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardTagSecret">The online tag shared secret from the matchmaker host (setup guide section A8). It is stored only in the card server's Configurations/server.json and is never shown or logged. Leave this empty to keep an already-set secret.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardBattleMode">When checked (the default), Save writes battleMode=0 in every setting group of 29\testmode_pcb.json so the cab uses online matchmaking. Other fields are left intact. A timestamped backup is made first, and the file is read back to confirm the change. If the svd1 checksum or version cannot be rewritten safely, the file is left unchanged and the rest of the wizard still saves.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WizardRunChecks">Test the card server port, try to reach the matchmaker over HTTP, and list the exact programs to allow in TinyWall or Windows Firewall. The launcher never changes firewall rules. An already-running patched card server is left running; a test-started process is stopped afterwards.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.Log">A running log of what the launcher did. Card values and secrets are never written here. A copy is also saved as exvs2-launcher.log next to the exe.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.CabMapping">The game folder name for this profile, plus the running game window title when the client is open (often PCB_03, PCB_14, and similar). The game sets that window name itself; this launcher cannot rename it. Use the cab name in the title bar to tell the windows apart.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.OpenWeb">Open the patched card server's WebUI in your default browser. The address is http://{card server host}:{port}/ (the Blazor app at / and /Cards). If the card server binds 0.0.0.0, localhost is used instead. Start the card server first if the page does not load.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.Controllers">Open the controller bindings editor. Pick a detected controller and rebind each game action (Rebind captures the next button press; Clear empties it). Combo keys, raw Name/Mode/DeviceId, and [xinput] settings are under Advanced. Product names come from HID / SetupAPI when Windows provides them; the stored Name= value stays the game-compatible string (for example Razer Panthera PS4). This is not a new input driver.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GameServerIni">Server= in config.ini and lmnfig.ini is the address the game and LM use to reach this cab's card server (host:port). It must match the card server address above or the LM fails with error 21-32. DisableSocketHook on the LM must stay False when more than one cab or LM runs on this PC.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.WriteServerJson">Write the matchmaker address (MatchUrl and StunHost) and, if you pasted one, the online tag secret to the card server's Configurations/server.json. A backup is made if the file changes. The tag secret is stored only in that file, is never shown in this window after you click Write, and is never logged. Leave the secret box empty to keep an existing secret.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerReload">Reload the bindings from config.ini (and SnowLauncher.ini if present) and discard unsaved changes in this window.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerWrite">Write the bindings to config.ini, and to SnowLauncher.ini if that option is on for this profile. A backup is made if a file changes. Close SnowLauncher and the game first. Unknown keys such as arcade bitmasks are left alone.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerClose">Close this window. Unsaved changes are discarded.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerDetected">Controllers currently visible through HID / SetupAPI product names, Raw Input, XInput slots 0–3, and the WinMM joystick API (DirectInput-style devices, including many fight sticks). Select one, then click Use selected controller, or Rebind next to an action and press the stick.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerRefresh">Scan again for connected pads and fight sticks. Plug the device in first.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.XInputEnabled">config.ini [xinput] EmulatedXInputEnabled. Leave this on unless a guide tells you otherwise.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.XInputMode">Optional [xinput] Mode, for example xinput-native. Leave empty if you are not using that path. This is not the same as the Mode key on a [controller] slot.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerDeviceId">0–15 selects one pad or stick; 16 means all devices. On [controller] this is the hook's DeviceId field.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerUseSelected">Copy the selected controller into the current tab (Controller 1 or 2). The INI Name= value stays the game-compatible stored name, even when the list shows a shorter product name.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerAdvanced">Combo buttons (AB, BC, AC, ABC), the raw Name / Mode / DeviceId fields, and [xinput] EmulatedXInputEnabled / Mode / DeviceId. Leave these alone unless you need them.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GraphicsClient">These columns write config.ini for the game client. Typical client resolution on this PC is 480p.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GraphicsLm">These columns write lmnfig.ini for the Live Monitor. Typical LM resolution on this PC is 144p.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GraphicsResolution">[display] Resolution= in config.ini or lmnfig.ini. Known values: 144p, 240p, 480p, 720p, 1080p, 2k, 4k, 8k. The box is editable if a file already has another token. Client and LM are independent. 8k is documented as needing Windowed and Borderless both on.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GraphicsWindowed">[config] Windowed in config.ini or lmnfig.ini. On means a window; off means fullscreen.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GraphicsBorderless">[config] BorderlessWindow in config.ini or lmnfig.ini. Use with Windowed for a borderless window.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GraphicsFramerate">[display] FramerateLimit in config.ini or lmnfig.ini. Limits the game's frame rate when the hook supports it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GraphicsOpening">[config] OpeningScreenSkip in config.ini or lmnfig.ini. Known values: None, SkipReminder, SkipBrand, SkipAll. SnowLauncher.ini is only updated for the observed SkipAll = 3 pairing.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GraphicsReload">Reload the graphics fields from config.ini and lmnfig.ini and discard unsaved changes in this section.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.GraphicsWrite">Write the graphics fields to config.ini and lmnfig.ini (and FramerateLimit / known OpeningScreenSkip to SnowLauncher.ini if that option is on). A backup is made if a file changes. Close the game, LM, and SnowLauncher first. Unknown keys are left alone. PCB_Resolution / LM_Resolution indexes are not written.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerLastPressed">The last button number heard from the selected controller, using the same 1-based ids written to config.ini (for a PS4-mode Razer Panthera, 1 is Square, 4 is Triangle, 10 is Options, and so on). Press a button here before you Rebind if you want to confirm the stick is being read.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.ControllerCancelBind">Stop waiting for a button press. The current binding is left unchanged.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.Keyboard">Open the keyboard bindings editor. Client and LM each have their own list. Rebind captures the next key press (8 seconds). Reset restores that action to the Infinite Boost pack default. This writes the existing INI keys; it is not a new input driver.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.KeyboardClient">These rows write config.ini [keyboard] for the game client (and [Game_Client_Keyboard] in SnowLauncher.ini if that option is on). Combo keys are expanded into the A=/B=/C= lists the hook reads.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.KeyboardLm">These rows write lmnfig.ini [keyboard] for the Live Monitor (and [Game_LM_Keyboard] in SnowLauncher.ini if that option is on). The hook in lmgrw.dll reads this file, not config.ini.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.KeyboardReload">Reload the bindings from config.ini and lmnfig.ini (and SnowLauncher.ini if present) and discard unsaved changes in this window.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.KeyboardWrite">Write the bindings to config.ini and lmnfig.ini, and to SnowLauncher.ini if that option is on for this profile. A backup is made if a file changes. Close SnowLauncher, the game, and the LM first. Unknown leftover keys such as ButtonR1 are left alone. Empty values are not written, because the hook can refuse to parse a blank key name.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.KeyboardClose">Close this window. Unsaved changes are discarded.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.KeyboardResetAll">Restore every action in this list to the Infinite Boost pack defaults in this window. Click Write bindings to save them.</sys:String>

  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanEnable">Optional n2n virtual LAN (off by default). When on, Start all first starts your edge.exe (Windows asks for administrator permission), waits for the TAP adapter to get its virtual IP, then writes that IP into config.ini and lmnfig.ini and starts the card server, LM, and client. The card server stays on this PC (127.0.0.1:28080). Everyone in the same community sees each other's LM, like one big LAN. Cabs on a virtual LAN do not mix with the public matchmaker queue. Turn it off to go back to your normal LAN IPs.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanEdge">Path to n2n's edge.exe that you downloaded or built yourself. The launcher does not ship or download it. Every player and the supernode should use the same n2n version (3.x with 3.x).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanSupernode">The n2n supernode as host:port, for example sn.example.net:7654. Your group shares this. Prefer a private supernode near the players.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanCommunity">The community (virtual LAN name) your group uses, up to 19 characters. Kept private: masked here and never written to the log.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanKey">The encryption key for the community (edge -k). It is saved only for this Windows user (encrypted with Windows DPAPI) in a separate file next to the launcher, never in the profiles file, and never shown or logged. Leave the box empty to keep the saved key.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanForgetKey">Delete the saved key for this profile.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanIp">Static: type a fixed address such as 10.77.0.11/24 that nobody else in the community uses. Assigned by supernode: the supernode picks one. DHCP: someone on the virtual LAN runs a DHCP server.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanAdvanced">MTU of the TAP adapter (edge -M). Default 1290 is safe on most connections.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanMetric">Interface metric of the TAP (edge -x). Windows sends LAN broadcasts on the lowest-metric adapter, so the default 1 lets the LM find the others on the TAP.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanAdapter">Only if this PC has more than one TAP adapter: its name from Windows network connections (edge -d). Leave empty otherwise.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanDescription">Optional short name other edges see for you (edge -I), up to 15 characters.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanMulticast">Accept multicast traffic (edge -E). Leave on for this game.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanRelay">Always send traffic through the supernode instead of directly (edge -S1). Only for testing hard NATs such as a phone hotspot; it adds delay.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanHeader">Encrypt n2n packet headers too (edge -H). Every player must use the same setting, and the supernode must know the community.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanStatus">TAP adapter, virtual IP, and peers from edge's management port (localhost UDP 5644). "Via supernode" peers have no direct path yet.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanConnect">Start edge now (with the administrator prompt) without starting the game. Start all does this for you.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="Tip.VlanDisconnect">Ask edge to exit cleanly. Close the LM and game first; they lose the virtual LAN.</sys:String>
</ResourceDictionary>
